  "slug": "noise-quantum-01a5",
  "summary": "Several preprints report sub-microsecond decoding and QEC-loop closure on FPGAs, making classical control latency a concrete design target for fault tolerance.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is co-design of the classical hardware that must keep pace with fault-tolerant quantum processors. On decoding, Rapids pairs a streaming neural decoder with FPGAs to decode surface-code syndromes in under a microsecond, reporting lower logical error rates than existing decoders. Pêlagos orchestrates relay belief-propagation decoding across multiple GPUs under real-time constraints. A survey organizes hierarchical decoding by decoder- and code-level hierarchy, platforms and latency trade-offs. CHEQ evaluates decoders concurrently on an FPGA via geometric-skip circuit-level noise sampling, matching Stim firing rates within 0.16%. QuFrame's control microarchitecture closes the QEC loop within 1 μs through real-time Pauli frame tracking. A second strand addresses platform-specific compilation and control: NEAT for AOD transport in neutral-atom arrays, an FPGA-based quantum data processing unit measuring NVQLink round-trip latency, Lean-verified QCCD co-design (QCCDCert) scaling to 512 qubits, MLIR-based circuit cutting (QWeaver), and fidelity-predictive multi-vendor QPU scheduling.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
